  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 AM Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...  Patchy dense fog and low clouds will reduce \nvisibility early this morning across portions of central NC, with \nthe lowest visibilities and most persistent fog focused across the \nSandhills and Coastal Plain.\n\nCalm winds and high low level moisture early this morning will help \nfog and low cloud development through daybreak. While patchy fog is \npossible farther west in the Piedmont, the thickest fog and lowest \ncloud cover will settle over eastern areas. Visibilities across the \nCoastal Plain could drop to a mile or less at times during the \nmorning commute, creating localized travel delays. Morning sunlight \nwill quickly burn off the moisture, returning clear skies to the \nentire region by mid morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Temperatures will trend well above average through \nFriday, with unseasonably hot, but not humid, afternoons.\n\nThe models indicate a mid-level sub-tropical ridge over the srn \nAppalachians will continue to strengthen while progressing across \nand offshore the Carolinas during the next couple of days.   \n\nAt the surface, a Canadian high and continental airmass now centered \nover the Middle Atlantic will progress offshore today, then extend \nfrom near Bermuda to the South Atlantic coast through Fri. Sly flow \naround the Bermuda high, and subsidence beneath the ridging aloft, \nwill contribute to warming temperatures through the week and what \nwill likely be the last few days of summerlike temperatures of the \nyear. While hotter, and gradually more humid/muggy by night, \nmoisture return will be shallow and susceptible to vertical \nadvection/mixing of lingering dry/continental within the ridge axis \nover NC, such that mixed/ afternoon dewpoints will probably only \nincrease from mid/upr 50s today to mostly mid 60s by Fri (with \nafternoon RH in the 30s to 40s percent).\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... Saturday will mark a transition to cloudy, much \ncooler, and periods of wet conditions that will continue through the \nweekend.\n\nA positively-tilted srn stream trough/shear axis will progress \nslowly across the Southeast and Carolinas this weekend, just \ndownstream and equatorward of a series of prominent shortwaves that \nwill progress through a broad trough that will become centered over \nern Canada. \n\nAt the surface, high pressure extending from near Bermuda to the \nSouth Atlantic coast will be displaced by a wavy frontal zone \nforecast to settle swd and across cntl NC on Sat (~3-6 hours faster \nthan previous model cycles). Following, progressive but favorably \nstrong and located high pressure for cold air damming initially over \nthe Great Lakes at 1028 mb, will ridge swd across the Middle \nAtlantic and Carolinas this weekend. \n\nGiven both earlier diurnal timing of the frontal passage on Sat \n(prior to peak heating) and otherwise limited low-level moisture and \nmid-level lapse rates, instability will be weak and unsupportive of \ndeep convection. A band of frontal showers and areas of light, \nanafrontal rain will instead probably result Sat. A better chance of \nsteadier rain will result Sat night-Sun in cntl NC, when and where \nsly and strengthening 850 mb flow will maximize isentropic lift atop \nthe CAD surface ridge. However, the lack of any substantial \nperturbations forecast within the aforementioned srn stream trough \naxis suggests frontal wave development will be limited, as will the \nrisk of any heavy rain. The isentropic upglide regime and saturated \nCAD airmass will nonetheless favor periods of at least light rain \nand drizzle late Sat through the weekend. As confidence in that \npattern (probably) increases in the coming days, forecast high \ntemperatures and diurnal ranges for the weekend will probably \nrequire substantial adjustments downward from currently forecast \nones. In fact, if the previously noted pattern occurs, temperatures \non Sun will probably be similar to the 60s F that occurred in the \nrecent cold air damming episode on the 23rd. \n\nA reinforcing cold front and following cP/Canadian surface high will \nprogress across the srn Middle Atlantic early next week.",
